00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.490 align:start position:0%
 
a very good morning to you thanks so  much for talking to us Jonathan Reynolds  let's start with the issue of Asylum  because the focus this week has very  much been on Asylum Seekers It's been  the issue of the week hasn't it what  would labor do what specifically would  you do to reduce the number of people  who are coming here by unsafe means  good morning yes and certainly has been  the the story of the week I mean the  entire approach from government to me  lacks any sense of basic competence or  compassion look there's no easy way to  address a crisis like this but  fundamentally what we would do is  process that backlog of claims I think  we have the worst  um of all Worlds at the minute where  people who who should be qualifying for  refuge in this country have to wait too  long for it in conditions which are  unacceptable and people who shouldn't be  in this country aren't processed quickly  enough and then told to leave the  country and crucially rather than have  gimmicks expensive gimmicks like the  Rwanda plan which clearly hasn't  deterred anyone from trying to come to  the UK we would spend that money on the  National Crime Agency on a specific sale  designed to stop those people smuggling  who are clearly involved in this trade  but that's the only way to tackle this  is competence tackling the claims that  come in in the way which frankly the  country did in the past and no longer  does and focusing our policing and Law  and Order resources on the criminal  gangs who are involved in it what about  opening up new kinds of routes into the  country that are safer that perhaps  could deter people from taking uh trips  in small boats across the channel open  up more safer legal routes  well I think you obviously must have  safer and legal routes to come to the UK  and particularly when they respond to  particular conflicts around the world  that have led to Refugee flaws coming  through that but I'll be frank I don't  think any of that would address the  problem if we have over a hundred  thousand uh people currently waiting for  their for their claims to be processed  as is the case today I mean that's up  from 40 000 five years ago I understand  just four percent of the claims relating  to people who came to the country last  year have been processed I mean how can  a system operate in that kind of  environment and I just don't feel the  government is interested in addressing  their own clearly basic failures in this  area and that's why you have this  never-ending set of gimmicks of frankly  things which won't make a difference  because they're trying to deflect from  the fact they've been unfortunately  completely incompetent on this  you talk about people being held in  unacceptable conditions uh talking to  the policing Minister earlier he said  that a number of people have been moved  out of Manson for example and into  hotels do you have Asylum seekers in  your constituency in hotels there  yes I mean people will be aware an area  like mine I represent a tame slider on  the east side of Manchester the how the  system works isn't it actually areas  um of the country where accommodation is  cheaper tend by this government to take  the burden of that now I've checked the  figures for my local area this week it's  actually less than I thought it would be  but this is a reality for people and  fundamentally comes down to the fact  that the government doesn't address the  basic cause of this in the way that it  should and that's that's the frustration  that people have with how the system  works so you you said that you've got  the figures of the people in in your  constituency are you happy to have more  migrants move to your constituency or  your constituents happy about having  people there and moved away from Kent  and away from processing centers like  manston well people around this country  and my constituents are no different to  understand the national nature of this  problem and are willing to play their  part in it I will be frank there is a  times resentment uh the fact that some  parts of the country are asked to take a  bigger share of the burden of this and  the government tends not to fund that in  the way that I think is required but the  cause of this what people want they want  the country to be a Bastion of  compassion to people who are genuinely  seeking refuge and need to be in this  country for that reason and they're very  happy to support that and actually local  organizations local Civic Society  particularly churches and Faith  communities play a massive role within  that but what they expect from  government is some competence is that  they're going to process these claims  they're going to make sure people are  you know given the compassion they  deserve or if they're not eligible to be  in the country process accordingly and  then leave the country and that is what  we haven't got at the minute and if it's  getting worse year on year the longer  the conservatives in power the worse  this problem gets well yes and and Kent  County councilors are have put forward  complaints about saying that that county  is taking an unnecessary uh level of  burden in terms of dealing with with  extra people would you say you were  happy to offer up your hotels and your  constituency as places that they should  travel to  look because this is like mine probably  doesn't have a significant number of of  hotels so it's it's not a practical  offer or or ability to to do it in that  way I can understand how local  representatives in in a place like  Kentfield they have to take a  considerable burden of this the  geography of the country is part of that  but again what they need is an approach  from government which is competent which  is compassionate which addresses the  root cause of this which isn't about  gimmicks or grandstanding or home  secretaries who frankly shouldn't be  restored to government trying to  grandstand for their own political  Ambitions you know this country will in  many countries in Europe have had this  this situation this issue to deal with  for some time but it hasn't in the last  few years in particular been dealt with  in the way it should be done and that is  at the cause of resentment I think the  government had some basic competence on  this you wouldn't see the resentments  and the pressures which we currently see  all around the country  I want to turn attention to to the  economy now if I can reports out this  morning that size well C the nuclear  plant for Suffolk that's been that's  being planned is now under review as the  government tries to cut spending Chris  Phil can confirm whether or not that was  true these are these are reports at this  stage but what do you make of that  we support a new nuclear power station  that site what really frustrates me at  the minute is I think you're seeing the  government flying kites every day some  expectation management around the Autumn  statement the reality is they've crushed  the economy and they've got to then take  corrective action because of it but what  would really frustrate me is to see Cuts  in the kind of Investments which  actually are crucial to the long-term  prosperity of the country I mean the one  thing I'll say Liz trust was right on  was that it's not just the last month  it's been a problem for the British  economy it's the last 12 years I mean  the economy hasn't grown as it should  have done and what you see with the  government flying these kites every day  in the media before they give us some  firm information is something which  further undermines confidence in the UK  I mean despite all the brilliant things  we have going for us in this country  that the UK is the country with the  lowest level of business investment in  the G7 and political instability leading  to economic instability has been a major  cause of that and for me the  conservatives will never be the answer  to that because Unfortunately they are  directly responsible for it size wall  for example has a has a 20 billion pound  price tag roughly labor obviously would  be Keen to back that still going ahead  um also making commitments on on tax for  example saying that you're committed to  the triple log are you in a position now  where you're committing to to higher  spending at a time when the chancellor  suddenly says that we can't afford it  well first of all we're going to make a  distinction between long-term investment  spending in the UK and day-to-day  Revenue spending now for us on  day-to-day spending we would not spend a  penny more than we raise in taxation we  don't believe in the conservative  approach that you can borrow money to  pay for unfunded tax cuts for the well  often include one of the reasons we're  in such a situation is where they've got  to so where we would for instance have  an expanded NHS Workforce improve the  backlogs in the NHS that money would  come from abolishing the non-dom rule of  bringing about 3.2 billion pounds we  would tax the income from private Equity  uh money differently we would not have  charitable status for private schools so  every time we we present a spending  commitment on day-to-day spending we'll  always say where that money came from  and that's why we can put them forward  those policies but particularly on  borrowing money you can borrow money to  address a crisis you can borrow money to  invest in the long term investments the  country needs but you can't borrow money  for day-to-day spending

